      "A dedicated research subagent audited Convex/SQLite parity, security/injection surfaces, orchestration-module test coverage, and AGENT_PROTOCOL docs clarity (the four empty-backlog fallback tiers) against current HEAD and found one real gap: /api/stripe/webhook was the sole public write route with no checkRateLimit gate, unlike every sibling (contact form, checkout create, commerce checkout create, billing portal, bootstrap-prices, unsubscribe) — AGENT_PROTOCOL itself documents that rollout as covering 'public write routes' generally and names the webhook receiver nowhere in that list. Concrete exposure this closed: the route reads the full raw body via an unbounded request.text() (deliberately exempt from the whole-body byte ceiling, since Stripe signs the raw payload — see src/lib/security/json-body.ts) and only rejects on signature mismatch *after* that read and an HMAC computation; the route is reachable by anyone (not only Stripe) at a conventional, documented path, so an unauthenticated scripted flood of large-bodied POSTs could force unbounded per-request memory/CPU work with no backoff, unlike every sibling route which rejects with 429 before doing comparable work. The size-cap exemption for this route (correct, since Stripe's signature covers the exact raw bytes) appears to have been read as a blanket 'this route is out of scope for hardening,' and the rate-limit half of the pass never got applied here independently. Independently re-verified before fixing (not the subagent's report as ground truth): grepped the route file and confirmed zero existing checkRateLimit/clientIpFromHeaders references; read every sibling public route (contact, checkout, commerce checkout, billing portal, bootstrap-prices, unsubscribe) and confirmed each already gates the same way; picked 120/60s specifically (not a reused DEFAULT_RATE_LIMIT) because a real merchant's event volume can legitimately burst well above a single-shopper-driven route's traffic, and because Stripe's own webhook retry policy (backoff + redelivery on non-2xx) makes an occasional false-positive 429 safe here in a way it would not be for, say, a checkout create request a human is waiting on. Added a route-test 429 case (mirroring the existing commerce-checkout precedent) that drives 121 requests from one synthetic x-forwarded-for IP and asserts the 121st never reaches signature verification (constructEvent called exactly 120 times) — confirmed this test file's existing afterEach vi.resetModules() call already gives every test (including pre-existing ones with no x-forwarded-for header) a fresh rate-limiter module instance per `await import(\"./route\")`, so no explicit resetRateLimiterForTests() plumbing was needed here (unlike commerce/checkout/route.test.ts, which persists module state across tests and does need it). Verified: npm test (2105 passed, 165 files — one net new test) + npm run lint (clean) + npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run build (tsc clean, all 31 routes generated) + npm run dev sanity check: curled /, /dashboard/leads, /preview/demo-cafe (all 200) and POSTed an empty body to /api/stripe/webhook (still correctly 501 not-configured before the rate-limit check even runs, since STRIPE_SECRET_KEY/STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET are unset in this dev environment — confirms the config-check gate still takes precedence and the new check didn't shift ordering), then stopped the dev server and confirmed no next-server process remained.",
